Basically:

Each Render Node will own an image for each frame in flight. Those images needs to be bound as
a sampled image in the descriptor sets, that way those images can always be sampled on a shader.

Then the process becomes:

- Node 1:
    - On the absolutely very first frame: Transition its own image (for the current frame index)
    from UNDEFINED to COLOR_ATTACHMENT_OPTIMAL.
    - For the frames after the absolutely very first frame: Transition its own
    image (for the current frame index) from SHADER_READ_ONLY_OPTIMAL to COLOR_ATTACHMENT_OPTIMAL.
    - Shader executes and writes color.
    - Transition image to SHADER_READ_ONLY_OPTIMAL.
- Node 2:
    - Transition its own image from SHADER_READ_ONLY_OPTIMAL to COLOR_ATTACHMENT_OPTIMAL.
    - Shader samples Node 1's image (which is safely in SHADER_READ_ONLY_OPTIMAL).
    - Shader writes color to Node 2's image.
    - Transition Node 2's image to SHADER_READ_ONLY_OPTIMAL.
- Node 3 (Present Node):
    - Acquire Swapchain Image.
    - Transition Swapchain Image from UNDEFINED to COLOR_ATTACHMENT_OPTIMAL.
    - Shader samples Node 2's image and draws a full-screen quad onto the Swapchain Image.
    - Transition Swapchain Image from COLOR_ATTACHMENT_OPTIMAL to PRESENT_SRC_KHR.
    - Submit command buffer and Present.

Repeat for next frame.
